  <content>
  <section><title>Lesson Narrative</title>
<para>This lesson focuses on factoring two different special products. Perfect square trinomials follow the pattern \(a^2
  \pm 2ab + b^2\) and factor to \((a + b)^2\) or \((a − b)^2\), depending on the sign of the middle term in the
  trinomial.</para>
<para>The difference of squares is represented by the binomial \(a^2 − b^2\) and factors to the conjugate pair \((a
  − b)(a + b)\).</para>
<para>Recognizing the patterns of these special products will allow for the easy factoring of these particular cases.</para>
<title>Learning Goals (Teacher Facing)</title>
<list>
  <item> Recognize the patterns of perfect square trinomials and factor them into the form \((a + b)^2\) or \((a −
    b)^2\). </item>
  <item> Recognize the difference of squares and factor them into conjugate pairs. </item>
</list>
<title>Learning Targets (Student Facing)</title>
<list>
  <item> Factor perfect square trinomials. </item>
  <item> Factor differences of squares. </item>
</list>
